  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 9bc4902ccea98cf44865aecb61fc1ff4, type: 3}
  m_Name: Dionise
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::SuspectData
  FirstName: Dionise
  LastName: Chkonia
  Nickname: Dio
  Occupation: Professor
  CharacterDescription: Stoic, calm and professional. Has a deep care for his students.
  CharacterArc: He begins as a dedicated educator. As the radiation spreads, he becomes
    nihilistic, questioning the value of human intellect and eventually viewing the
    "unmaking" of the world as a necessary cleansing of man-made folly.
  DateOfBirth: 09/03/1953
  Sex: Male
  EntryPermitExpiryDate: 12 JUN
  IsResident: 1
  GivesPaperwork: 1
  handwritingFont: {fileID: 11400000, guid: f8e89ead54f8523428cc2e34f005bb5e, type: 2}
  startingInfectionScore: 0
  dailyInfectionProgression: 5
  IDPhoto: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 8de7601dc6d14af43838e8b13cedd6a2, type: 3}
  CharacterPrefab: {fileID: 5164911391202306748, guid: a27411530d2c9aa4eab6a559a8832b11, type: 3}
  entryReasons:
    earlyDaysReasons:
    - Returning to campus for the winter term.
    - Resident returning from academic leave.
    - Retrieving essential research materials.
    midDaysReasons:
    - Checking the structural integrity of the lab.
    - Escaping the fallout for university shelter.
    - Meeting with the dean regarding safety.
    finalDaysReasons:
    - Returning to my registered faculty housing.
    - Seeking refuge.
    - Fulfilling final residency requirements.
  invalidEntryReasons:
    earlyDaysReasons:
    - Lecturing a class of empty chairs.
    - Grading papers written in human blood.
    - Searching for a door that leads to 1890.
    midDaysReasons:
    - Teaching the atoms how to stop spinning.
    - Discussing philosophy with the radiation.
    - Counting the seconds until the sun breaks.
    finalDaysReasons:
    - To help the shadows finish their dinner.
    - Attending the graduation ceremony for the dead.
    - I need to go back and erase the chalkboard.
  entryDialogues:
    lastVerdict: 0
    dialoguesEarlyDays:
    - "Good day, officer. I\u2019m just returning from the university archives."
    - "The students are anxious. I\u2019m heading back to provide some stability."
    - Stoicism is the only logical response to a crisis, wouldn't you agree?
    dialoguesMidDays:
    - The books are starting to feel... heavy. As if the ink is rotting.
    - I saw a bird fall from the sky today. It didn't even scream.
    - Human intellect is a very fragile glass, and the hammer is coming.
    dialoguesFinalDays:
    - The fire is a beautiful editor. It deletes all our mistakes.
    - Why check the papers? Soon, the ash will be our only record.
    - I am going home to watch the horizon dissolve into white.
  exitDialoguesPassed:
    lastVerdict: 1
    dialoguesEarlyDays:
    - Thank you. Stay focused. Order is our only defense.
    dialoguesMidDays:
    - "Go home, officer. While there\u2019s still a home to go to."
    dialoguesFinalDays:
    - "Enter the tomb. It\u2019s warmer than the street."
  exitDialoguesQuarantined:
    lastVerdict: 2
    dialoguesEarlyDays:
    - A setback, but perhaps a necessary precaution for the group.
    dialoguesMidDays:
    - Isolation. A perfect laboratory for watching one's mind fray.
    dialoguesFinalDays:
    - Lock me away. The unmaking happens everywhere at once.
  exitDialoguesKilled:
    lastVerdict: 3
    dialoguesEarlyDays:
    - A very... unrefined solution to a complex problem.
    dialoguesMidDays:
    - And so the curriculum ends. I expected a more elegant finale.
    dialoguesFinalDays:
    - Yes... simplify the equation. Zero is the only true answer.
  quarantineExitLines:
  - Observed. Contained. The scientific method applied to the scientist. How fitting.
  - Four walls and time to think. A man of lesser character would struggle.
  - A cage for a man who has already let everything go. What excellent symmetry.
  killExitLines:
  - Reduce. Simplify. Erase. Yes... that is the correct theorem.
  - "The experiment concludes... cleanly... I expected nothing... less."
  - "One more variable... removed from an already... simple equation..."
  voiceAudioClips: []
  questionResponses:
  - question: Where are you coming from?
    earlyDaysAnswer: The town square. I had some business to sort out.
    midDaysAnswer: The old district. Things are cheaper there now that most people
      have left.
    finalDaysAnswer: I've just been walking. I do that when I can't sleep.
    mismatchEarlyDaysAnswer: The university. Full morning of lectures. The students
      were attentive. A fine day for learning.
    mismatchMidDaysAnswer: The library. I go every morning without exception. Routine
      is the fortress of the rational mind.
    mismatchFinalDaysAnswer: The university. Teaching continues. The students need
      structure, now more than ever. Especially now.
  - question: Have you been experiencing any strange symptoms lately?
    earlyDaysAnswer: I get headaches sometimes. But I always have.
    midDaysAnswer: I've been losing track of time. Hours disappear. I'll look up
      and it's dark.
    finalDaysAnswer: My mouth tastes like copper. Has for about two weeks now.
    mismatchEarlyDaysAnswer: None. My health is excellent. I track it rigorously.
      Self-monitoring is simply good epistemology.
    mismatchMidDaysAnswer: Time? I account for every minute. A man who loses time
      loses himself. I have never lost a minute.
    mismatchFinalDaysAnswer: Nothing at all. No metallic taste. No fatigue. I feel,
      frankly, very lucid. Remarkably so.
  - question: Who do you live with?
    earlyDaysAnswer: My wife and our two children.
    midDaysAnswer: My wife. The kids are staying with her mother outside the city.
    finalDaysAnswer: My wife. She's not well, but she's still with me.
    mismatchEarlyDaysAnswer: Alone. I find solitude necessary for sustained intellectual
      output. My wife understood that eventually.
    mismatchMidDaysAnswer: My wife and both children. All four of us, together. The
      family unit remains intact. As it should.
    mismatchFinalDaysAnswer: Alone. My wife left the city some time ago. I don't
      recall exactly when. Time is... imprecise lately.
  idleBarks:
    earlyDays:
    - Stoicism is the only logical response to a crisis. Would you not agree?
    - My students keep asking me what comes next. I tell them what history says. They do not like the answer.
    - Order is its own kind of courage, officer. You are practicing it right now.
    midDays:
    - Human intellect is a very fragile glass, and the hammer is coming.
    - I gave a lecture to an empty hall yesterday. I finished it. Habit is the skeleton of civilization.
    - The books are starting to feel heavy. As if the ink itself is rotting.
    finalDays:
    - Why check the papers? Soon the ash will be the only record anyone keeps.
    - The fire is a beautiful editor. It deletes all our mistakes at once.
    - Zero is the only honest number in a collapsing system. Everything reduces to it.
  IDNumber: 7262765
